\section{Higher Contamination Details}
\label{app:rho06}

At $\rho{=}0.6$ (seed\,42 only), degradation rates compared to $\rho{=}0.4$:
adaptive $+$0.087, uniform $+$0.072, grid $b{=}0.5$ $+$0.053, ESS-only $+$0.040.
The fixed exponent $b{=}0.5$ degrades least among effective methods, suggesting $\tau{=}0.8$ is slightly too aggressive at higher contamination.
ESS-only (PPL 17.770) falls within 0.004 of uniform (17.766); with more synthetic samples per batch the ESS floor binds earlier, preventing the solver from raising $b$ enough to suppress them effectively.

